83e3e1f37795404fa64f4050918ca5b419d183d"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:T10","usage":{"input_tokens":78813,"output_tokens":2052,"requests":5}},{"kind":"success","lineage":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"03411240010263d4612fdb9af9cb5876735a0f086145570634cbe8cc4bf04e15"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:G7","usage":{"input_tokens":120508,"output_tokens":2644,"requests":5}},{"kind":"success","lineage":{"dependencies":[],"digest":"1056fb0d572c1453cd4396dad727f72407d1047edc0ee4a491f095c55ba549ec"},"namespace_version":1,"reason":null,"unit_id":"agent:T3","usage":{"input_tokens":63423,"output_tokens":3188,"requests":5}}],"version":1},"findings":[{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Correct the image/layer-share enforcement lever: `--keep-storage` bounds only the BuildKit cache (part 1), not the image store; derive image retention from autodeploy's existing image-reclamation path (mcp_retire_image / orphan-tag sweep / dangling prune) tied to the 15 GiB budget, and reserve `--keep-storage` for the 5 GiB BuildKit share only.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.4,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: 'Image/layer share (15 GiB) — cap-aware prune in autodeploy.sh, deriving --keep-storage from the same budget so prune and daemon GC cannot disagree.'","AC4: 'autodeploy.sh derives --keep-storage from the same budget'","autodeploy.sh:132 '# buildkit cache hard cap (docker builder prune --keep-storage)' and autodeploy.sh:377 'docker builder prune -f --keep-storage \"$BUILD_CACHE_KEEP\"' — --keep-storage is a BuildKit-only flag","autodeploy.sh:380 'docker image prune -f' takes NO --keep-storage; image reclamation is done via mcp_retire_image / orphan-tag sweep (autodeploy.sh:516-560), not a storage ceiling","Plan itself concedes 'Docker exposes no native total-image ceiling; retention is the supported lever' — but then names --keep-storage, which retains the build cache, not images"],"finding":"The image/layer share (15 GiB) enforcement mechanism names `--keep-storage`, which is a BuildKit (`docker builder prune`) flag that controls the build-cache share, not the image/layer store — so it cannot enforce the image share it is assigned to.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f0d81a05ca085cb4c","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Design § 'Image/layer share (15 GiB) — cap-aware prune'; Acceptance Criteria line 4","norm_id":"n6c35135577afa3a8","priority":1.0,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A runaway image set saturates the 15 GiB image share while the BuildKit cache is small; tuning --keep-storage down does nothing to the images, so the image share is never actually enforced and the alarm-only path is all that remains — defeating the 'cap-aware prune' claim.","The single-budget invariant is violated: --keep-storage derived from the 15 GiB image share and the 5 GiB BuildKit share both target the SAME build-cache lever, so 'prune and daemon GC cannot disagree' is vacuously about the wrong bytes."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Split the two levers explicitly in the design and AC4: (1) BuildKit share (5 GiB) → `builder.gc` + `docker builder prune --keep-storage`; (2) image/layer share (15 GiB) → the existing tag-retention/orphan-sweep in autodeploy (mcp_retire_image, mcp_image_tags sweep, `docker image prune`) sized against the 15 GiB budget — not `--keep-storage`. State plainly that Docker has no image-store ceiling so retention (count/age of tagged releases + dangling prune), not a byte flag, is the lever.","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"contradicts_reality","dod_uncertifiable":"certification_cannot_prove","internal_conflict":"high","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"high","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Replace the mismatched `--keep-storage` image-cap lever: specify the actual image-store retention mechanism (e.g. count/age-based `docker image prune -a --filter` or the existing mcp per-release retirement path) and reserve `--keep-storage` for the BuildKit share only.","cohort":["T3"],"criteria":["T3"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.1,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan Part 2: 'Image/layer share (15 GiB) — cap-aware prune in autodeploy.sh, deriving --keep-storage from the same budget'","autodeploy.sh:377 `docker builder prune -f --keep-storage \"$BUILD_CACHE_KEEP\"` — the flag is only ever a BuildKit builder-prune flag","autodeploy.sh:132 comment: 'buildkit cache hard cap (docker builder prune --keep-storage)'","infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md:704 confirms --keep-storage pairs with `docker builder prune`, while image cleanup uses plain `docker image prune -f`","docker image prune / docker system prune expose no --keep-storage flag (only -a/-f/--filter) — MISMATCH at the flag level"],"finding":"The plan names `--keep-storage` as the lever enforcing the 15 GiB IMAGE/LAYER share, but `--keep-storage` is exclusively a `docker builder prune` (BuildKit cache) flag and bounds the build cache (Part 1's 5 GiB share), not the image store; `docker image prune` has no such flag. As written, the image/layer cap has no working enforcement mechanism.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fa0b585b43e82c95d","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Design > 'Image/layer share (15 GiB) — cap-aware prune'; AC line 'autodeploy.sh derives --keep-storage from the same budget'","norm_id":"n2382fd0625d0b9f2","priority":1.0,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A runaway tagged-image set fills the 15 GiB image/layer share; the derived --keep-storage passed to builder prune only trims the BuildKit cache and never bounds the image store, so the cap is silently un-enforced after ship."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Rename Part 2's lever from '--keep-storage' to an explicit image-retention policy (tag-count/age filters on `docker image prune -a --filter`, or budget-driven mcp_reconcile retention), keeping `--keep-storage` scoped to Part 1's BuildKit share; update the AC line accordingly.","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"na","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"no","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"module","debt_impact":"medium","divergent_implementation":"contradicts_reality","dod_uncertifiable":"certification_cannot_prove","internal_conflict":"high","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"high","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"none"}}},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State the failure disposition of the version-probe fallback: what surfaces (a WARN/metric/rendered-schema field) when the engine version cannot be determined and the script defaults to the modern schema, so 'cap silently absent' is observable rather than mistaken for a working cap.","cohort":["E3","E5","G5","T12","T5b","T6","T9","necessity","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.portability"],"criteria":["project.failure-disposition-contract"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.3044,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["'falling back to dockerd --version on first boot, then the modern schema'","'The wrong key is silently IGNORED — the cap would simply not exist.'"],"finding":"The daemon.json render path has a version-probe fallback (docker version --format, else dockerd --version, else assume the modern schema) whose failure disposition is not stated: if the probe misreads or cannot read the engine version and defaults to the modern schema on an engine below 25.0, the plan itself notes the wrong key is 'silently IGNORED' — so the cap silently does not exist, which is exactly the outage the plan protects against, yet nothing alarms on it.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f0f3ce055f99667ad","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"Design > 'The daemon.json schema is version-dependent, so it is PROBED, not assumed'","norm_id":"n4008f017b7b3444f","priority":0.5956,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["First-boot or degraded host where version detection fails, engine <25.0, script writes maxUsedSpace, BuildKit GC never fires, cache grows uncapped and no alarm distinguishes 'cap absent' from 'cap working'."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Emit a probe-outcome signal (e.g. a resolved-schema value in --print-json and a WARN when version is undetermined) and have the print/merge path fail-closed or flag the guessed schema so an unknown engine version is not silently mapped to a possibly-ignored key.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.8889,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"medium","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"medium"}}},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Ground the runbook 'ban on deletion under /var/lib/docker' in the design body (state WHY manual rm is unsafe and the daemon-level reclaim to use instead).","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5a","T5e","T7"],"criteria":["E1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.7614,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["AC: 'The runbook documents ... plus a ban on deletion under /var/lib/docker.'","Design body discusses orphaned overlay2 and 'a daemon-level reclaim' but never states a deletion ban rationale"],"finding":"The runbook acceptance criterion requires 'a ban on deletion under /var/lib/docker', but no design-body section describes or motivates this ban — it appears only in the AC.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fe04dcd1daac7cb2b","impact":0.264,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria — runbook bullet ('plus a ban on deletion under /var/lib/docker')","norm_id":"n1f62f63bd155a86b","priority":0.1886,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["Implementer writes runbook and is unsure why deletion is banned or what the safe alternative is"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add a sentence in the 'Orphaned overlay2' design note explaining that manual deletion under /var/lib/docker corrupts the layer store, hence the daemon-level reclaim, and that the runbook bans it.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.7143,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"na","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"na","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"no","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"no","path_reachable":"na","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"none","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"low","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"none","internal_conflict":"low","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"low","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"easy","silent_vs_self_revealing":"self_revealing","undecomposed":"none","vague_directive":"low"}}},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State the du timeout / bound value (e.g. a stated seconds timeout under the 5-min probe interval) so the 'bounded' du is not left to executor judgment.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5a","T5e","T7"],"criteria":["E2","T5a"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.3525,"drop_reason":"novelty","evidence":["'a bounded du -sx over /var/lib/docker'","'A failed/timed-out probe publishes nothing'"],"finding":"The probe relies on a du timeout ('A failed/timed-out probe publishes nothing') but the plan does not state the timeout value or the du block-count bound, leaving a tunable unspecified for the executor.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f96d86373896ef7